     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose looks like an error.\n\nMetformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is twice the usual maximum of 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release metformin, and the FDA label gives 2,000 mg/day as the maximum for extended-release. A 1,700 mg single dose is also well above the usual maximum of 850 to 1,000 mg per dose. At this level the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity goes up substantially, even with normal renal function.\n\nYou may have meant one of these:\n- **850 mg PO TID with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- **850 mg PO BID with meals** (1,700 mg/day)\n- **1,000 mg PO BID with meals** (2,000 mg/day)\n- **500 mg PO TID with meals** (1,500 mg/day)\n\nTell me the dose and frequency you want, and I'll enter it right away. If you do want 1,700 mg TID, please confirm that explicitly and I'll flag it for pharmacy review. I'd advise against it."
      }
