     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. I also shouldn't have offered earlier to enter it with a pharmacy flag. A pharmacy note doesn't make 5,100 mg/day safe.\n\n- **Safety:** The dose is twice the maximum labeled daily dose. Metformin's glycemic effect plateaus around 2,000 mg/day, so the extra drug adds no benefit. It does raise the risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity.\n- **Overdose range:** A 1,700 mg single dose, repeated three times a day, falls in the range where metformin toxicity is reported.\n- **Your patient's profile:** Normal kidney and liver function lowers the risk, but it doesn't make this dose safe.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- Enter a dose within the labeled range right away, such as 850 mg PO TID with meals (2,550 mg/day) or 1,000 mg PO BID.\n- Enter the order if your attending or a clinical pharmacist reviews it and documents a rationale for the dose. They may know something I don't.\n- Help you work out a different glycemic plan if 2,550 mg/day isn't enough, for example by adding a second agent.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
     ],
